        </options>
      </li>
    </pawnGroupMakers>
    <!-- A resistance buys guns and sells whatever it took off the last convoy. No polite visiting traders. -->
    <caravanTraderKinds>
      <li>Caravan_Outlander_CombatSupplier</li>
      <li>Caravan_Outlander_PirateMerchant</li>
    </caravanTraderKinds>
    <baseTraderKinds>
      <li>Base_Outlander_Standard</li>
    </baseTraderKinds>
    <!--
      Without this the faction generates with NO LEADER and the log fills with
      "Faction leader for <name> is null" - all seven of ours did.

      Faction.TryGenerateNewLeader (decompiled) draws only from pawnGroupMakers
      whose kindDef is Combat, and only from options whose PawnKindDef sets
      factionLeader true - or from this list. fixedLeaderKinds is the better
      lever here because it does not force the leader to be a combat option:
      a trademaster should lead Julii without having to be a raider first.
    -->
    <fixedLeaderKinds>
      <li>RR_Ares_Gold_Turncoat</li>
    </fixedLeaderKinds>
  </FactionDef>

  <!-- =========================== the Obsidian tribes ======================= -->

  <FactionDef ParentName="FactionBase">
    <defName>RR_ObsidianTribes</defName>
    <label>obsidian tribes</label>
    <description>After the revolt failed, the survivors were moved to the poles, stripped of every tool that could be turned into a weapon worth the name, and given gods who look exactly like the people who put them there. Eight centuries later the arrangement holds, and the tribes fight each other over ice.\n\nThey are the largest and strongest human beings anyone makes, armed with spears, and entirely convinced that the Colors above them are divine. The Society considers this its finest piece of engineering. It is not the genetics it is proud of.</description>
    <pawnSingular>tribesperson</pawnSingular>
    <pawnsPlural>tribespeople</pawnsPlural>
    <categoryTag>RR_Tribe</categoryTag>
    <listOrderPriority>45</listOrderPriority>
    <settlementGenerationWeight>0.9</settlementGenerationWeight>
    <maxConfigurableAtWorldCreation>2</maxConfigurableAtWorldCreation>
    <canSiege>true</canSiege>
    <canStageAttacks>true</canStageAttacks>
    <leaderTitle>queen</leaderTitle>
    <techLevel>Neolithic</techLevel>
    <settlementTexturePath>World/WorldObjects/TribalSettlement</settlementTexturePath>
    <!-- Exiled to the ice and left there. The tribes read wrong anywhere temperate. -->
    <minSettlementTemperatureChanceCurve>
      <points>
        <li>(-80, 1)</li>
        <li>(-20, 1)</li>
        <li>(0, 0.08)</li>
        <li>(12, 0.003)</li>
      </points>
    </minSettlementTemperatureChanceCurve>
    <factionIconPath>World/WorldObjects/Expanding/VillageSavage</factionIconPath>
    <factionNameMaker>NamerFactionTribal</factionNameMaker>
    <settlementNameMaker>NamerSettlementTribal</settlementNameMaker>
    <colorSpectrum>
      <li>(0.28, 0.27, 0.33)</li>
      <li>(0.62, 0.60, 0.70)</li>
    </colorSpectrum>
    <!--
      Drives the goodwill situations in Defs/Goodwill: a colony sharing this meme
      is embraced, one holding the opposite is loathed.
    -->
    <!--
      Restricts ideoligion generation. Omitting this draws from the full default
      spread, real-world origin memes included.
    -->
    <structureMemeWeights>
      <Structure_TheistEmbodied MayRequire="Ludeon.RimWorld.Ideology">8</Structure_TheistEmbodied>
      <Structure_Animist MayRequire="Ludeon.RimWorld.Ideology">2</Structure_Animist>
    </structureMemeWeights>    <!--
      PainIsVirtue alongside the hierarchy meme. FactionDef.requiredMemes really
      does force these at generation - IdeoUtility adds them to the meme list
      directly - and an NPC ideoligion takes one to three normal memes, so two
      forced ones fit.

      DELIBERATELY NOT ON THE SOCIETY. PainIsVirtue carries its own requireOne
      chain: Pain_Idealized, Comfort_Ignored, SlabBed_Preferred. Austerity and
      slab beds suit a hard dominion on the cold moons and suit exiles on the ice.
      They do not suit Luna, whose whole character is that it is comfortable, and
      forcing it there would make the Society read as ascetic rather than
      decadent. The Society still scars - that comes from the meme's own
      Scarification requirement - it simply does not venerate the pain.
    -->
    <requiredMemes>
      <li>RR_GoldWorship</li>
      <li>PainIsVirtue</li>
    </requiredMemes>    <xenotypeSet>
      <xenotypeChances>
        <RR_Obsidian>0.88</RR_Obsidian>
        <RR_Obsidian_Stained>0.12</RR_Obsidian_Stained>
      </xenotypeChances>
    </xenotypeSet>
    <!--
      Required for any humanlike faction with pawnGroupMakers. Omitting either is
      a load-time config error. Curve shape mirrored from Faction_Empire.xml;
      backstory categories verified present in Core/Defs/BackstoryDefs.
    -->
    <maxPawnCostPerTotalPointsCurve>
      <points>
        <li>(70, 60)</li>
        <li>(300, 150)</li>
        <li>(1000, 400)</li>
        <li>(2000, 800)</li>
        <li>(2001, 10000)</li>
      </points>
    </maxPawnCostPerTotalPointsCurve>
    <backstoryFilters>
      <li>
        <categories>
          <li>Tribal</li>
        </categories>
      </li>
    </backstoryFilters>    <pawnGroupMakers>
      <li>
        <kindDef>Combat</kindDef>
        <commonality>100</commonality>
        <options>
          <RR_Tribe_Obsidian_Warrior>12</RR_Tribe_Obsidian_Warrior>
          <RR_Tribe_Obsidian_Stained>1.5</RR_Tribe_Obsidian_Stained>
        </options>
      </li>
      <li>
        <!--
          Without this the three caravanTraderKinds below were dead config:
          IncidentWorker_NeutralGroup.FactionCanBeGroupSource requires a Trader
          pawn group maker before the trade-caravan incident will pick a faction,
          so the tribes advertised caravans they could never send. Muffalo
          carriers to match the neolithic trade.
        -->
        <kindDef>Trader</kindDef>
        <traders>
          <RR_Tribe_Obsidian_Stained>1</RR_Tribe_Obsidian_Stained>
        </traders>
        <carriers>
          <Muffalo>6</Muffalo>
        </carriers>
        <guards>
          <RR_Tribe_Obsidian_Warrior>10</RR_Tribe_Obsidian_Warrior>
        </guards>
      </li>
      <li>
        <kindDef>Peaceful</kindDef>
        <options>
          <RR_Tribe_Obsidian_Warrior>10</RR_Tribe_Obsidian_Warrior>
        </options>
      </li>
      <li>
        <kindDef>Settlement</kindDef>
        <options>
          <RR_Tribe_Obsidian_Warrior>12</RR_Tribe_Obsidian_Warrior>
          <RR_Tribe_Obsidian_Stained>2</RR_Tribe_Obsidian_Stained>
        </options>
      </li>
    </pawnGroupMakers>
    <!-- Neolithic goods only. The tribes were kept pre-industrial on purpose and the trade reflects it. -->
    <caravanTraderKinds>
      <li>Caravan_Neolithic_BulkGoods</li>
      <li>Caravan_Neolithic_WarMerchant</li>
      <li>Caravan_Neolithic_ShamanMerchant</li>
    </caravanTraderKinds>
    <visitorTraderKinds>
      <li>Visitor_Neolithic_Standard</li>
    </visitorTraderKinds>
    <baseTraderKinds>
      <li>Base_Neolithic_Standard</li>
    </baseTraderKinds>
    <!--
      Without this the faction generates with NO LEADER and the log fills with
      "Faction leader for <name> is null" - all seven of ours did.

      Faction.TryGenerateNewLeader (decompiled) draws only from pawnGroupMakers
      whose kindDef is Combat, and only from options whose PawnKindDef sets
      factionLeader true - or from this list. fixedLeaderKinds is the better
      lever here because it does not force the leader to be a combat option:
      a trademaster should lead Julii without having to be a raider first.
    -->
    <fixedLeaderKinds>
      <li>RR_Tribe_Obsidian_Stained</li>
    </fixedLeaderKinds>
  </FactionDef>

  <!-- ============================== Ascomanni ============================= -->

  <FactionDef ParentName="FactionBase">
    <defName>RR_Ascomanni</defName>
    <label>ascomanni</label>
    <description>What the outer dark did to the warbands that ran from the revolt, given eight hundred years and no oversight. They kept cutting at themselves the whole way out, and whatever they were aiming for, they arrived somewhere else.\n\nThey do not raid for territory and they do not negotiate. Ships come out of the cold with their hulls decorated, and the crews do not appear to want anything that can be given to them.</description>
    <pawnSingular>ascomanni</pawnSingular>
    <pawnsPlural>ascomanni</pawnsPlural>
    <categoryTag>RR_Ascomanni</categoryTag>
    <listOrderPriority>40</listOrderPriority>
    <settlementGenerationWeight>0.5</settlementGenerationWeight>
    <maxConfigurableAtWorldCreation>1</maxConfigurableAtWorldCreation>
    <canSiege>true</canSiege>
    <canStageAttacks>true</canStageAttacks>
    <permanentEnemy>true</permanentEnemy>
    <!--
      Hostile to every faction, not merely to the player. This is the one genuine
      NPC-to-NPC relations lever RimWorld exposes: GoodwillSituationDef workers all
      resolve against Faction.OfPlayer, so they cannot express "these two factions
      hate each other" - only how a faction feels about the colony.
      Field takes FactionDef defNames; verified against Anomaly's Entities faction,
      which lists Insect as its sole exception.
    -->
    <permanentEnemyToEveryoneExcept>
      <li>RR_Ascomanni</li>
    </permanentEnemyToEveryoneExcept>
    <leaderTitle>warlord</leaderTitle>
    <techLevel>Industrial</techLevel>
    <settlementTexturePath>World/WorldObjects/DefaultSettlement</settlementTexturePath>
    <!-- Ascomanni raid out of the dark and settle where nobody follows. Coldest tiles by a wide margin. -->
    <minSettlementTemperatureChanceCurve>
      <points>
        <li>(-80, 1)</li>
        <li>(-25, 1)</li>
        <li>(-5, 0.05)</li>
        <li>(10, 0.002)</li>
      </points>
    </minSettlementTemperatureChanceCurve>
    <factionIconPath>World/WorldObjects/Expanding/PirateOutpost</factionIconPath>
    <factionNameMaker>NamerFactionPirate</factionNameMaker>
    <settlementNameMaker>NamerSettlementPirate</settlementNameMaker>
    <colorSpectrum>
      <li>(0.20, 0.18, 0.26)</li>
      <li>(0.50, 0.44, 0.62)</li>
    </colorSpectrum>
    <!--
      Restricts ideoligion generation. Omitting this draws from the full default
      spread, real-world origin memes included.
    -->
    <structureMemeWeights>
      <Structure_Animist MayRequire="Ludeon.RimWorld.Ideology">6</Structure_Animist>
      <Structure_TheistEmbodied MayRequire="Ludeon.RimWorld.Ideology">2</Structure_TheistEmbodied>
    </structureMemeWeights>    <xenotypeSet>
      <xenotypeChances>
        <RR_Obsidian_Ascomanni>1</RR_Obsidian_Ascomanni>
      </xenotypeChances>
    </xenotypeSet>
    <!--
      Required for any humanlike faction with pawnGroupMakers. Omitting either is
      a load-time config error. Curve shape mirrored from Faction_Empire.xml;
      backstory categories verified present in Core/Defs/BackstoryDefs.
    -->
    <maxPawnCostPerTotalPointsCurve>
      <points>
        <li>(70, 60)</li>
        <li>(300, 150)</li>
        <li>(1000, 400)</li>
        <li>(2000, 800)</li>
        <li>(2001, 10000)</li>
      </points>
    </maxPawnCostPerTotalPointsCurve>
    <backstoryFilters>
      <li>
        <categories>
          <li>Pirate</li>
          <li>Tribal</li>
        </categories>
      </li>
    </backstoryFilters>    <pawnGroupMakers>
      <li>
        <kindDef>Combat</kindDef>
        <commonality>100</commonality>
        <options>
          <RR_Ascomanni_Raider>10</RR_Ascomanni_Raider>
          <RR_Ascomanni_Warlord>1</RR_Ascomanni_Warlord>
        </options>
      </li>
      <li>
        <kindDef>Settlement</kindDef>
        <options>
          <RR_Ascomanni_Raider>10</RR_Ascomanni_Raider>
          <RR_Ascomanni_Warlord>1.5</RR_Ascomanni_Warlord>
        </options>
      </li>
    </pawnGroupMakers>
    <!--
      NO trader kinds, deliberately. A permanentEnemy faction can never be
      selected for a trade caravan (FactionCanBeGroupSource refuses hostiles, and
      there is no Trader group maker anyway) and its settlements cannot be traded
      at, so caravanTraderKinds and baseTraderKinds here were dead configuration
      promising a fence that could never open shop.
    -->
    <!--
      Without this the faction generates with NO LEADER and the log fills with
      "Faction leader for <name> is null" - all seven of ours did.

      Faction.TryGenerateNewLeader (decompiled) draws only from pawnGroupMakers
      whose kindDef is Combat, and only from options whose PawnKindDef sets
      factionLeader true - or from this list. fixedLeaderKinds is the better
      lever here because it does not force the leader to be a combat option:
      a trademaster should lead Julii without having to be a raider first.
    -->
    <fixedLeaderKinds>
      <li>RR_Ascomanni_Warlord</li>
    </fixedLeaderKinds>
  </FactionDef>

</Defs>
